From e2f966ba0f3edf4efbe02c0841c13cf7f2d9d4fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Mohamed R. Khalil" <m.radwan.khalil@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 01:36:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(v2): key the process config cache by every input it was
 built from

The process-level config cache could replay artifacts built from different inputs. The agent snapshot ignored the OMO config, so an instance with other disabled agents, models or prompts got the first instance's agents. The MCP cache watched two files, neither of which is one of the four the loader reads, and ignored the loader's cwd. The command cache ignored the user command and skill directories. The project fingerprint saw only top-level entries, so a file added or edited below an existing source directory was missed, and the per-handler snapshot hid even the changes it did see. Ancestor directories, which the project loaders also search, were not considered at all.

Every key now covers its inputs: the OpenCode config slice, a digest of the OMO config and of the loaded plugin components, content fingerprints (path, mtime and size of every file, six levels deep) of the user-level and project source directories and of the sources contributed by ancestors, the targets of file:// prompt references, and for MCP the loader context, its four files, the env allowlist and the environment. The per-handler snapshot uses the same key. A source tree too large to fingerprint is not served from the process cache. Sibling chat folders still share one snapshot.
